{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.54,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Make the closed-port probe deterministic (e.g., hold the socket open and probe a port known-refused, or retry/skip on reassignment) instead of relying on a released ephemeral port staying closed.","criteria":["edge-cases","maintainability"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["tests/unit/_healthcheck_oracles.py:86: def _reserve_closed_port() -> int:","tests/unit/_healthcheck_oracles.py:87:     with socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM) as sock:","tests/unit/_healthcheck_oracles.py:89:         return int(sock.getsockname()[1])","The socket is closed on `with` exit before `_run(_rewrite_health_url(argv, _reserve_closed_port()))` executes (line 166), so nothing guarantees the port remains unbound during the probe."],"finding":"`_reserve_closed_port()` binds an ephemeral port, closes the socket, and returns the port number on the assumption it stays closed; between the close and the subprocess probe the OS may reassign that port to another listener, so the 'closed listener must fail' assertion can flake (or, worse, pass for the wrong reason).","id":"0","impact":0.27,"location":"tests/unit/_healthcheck_oracles.py:86","norm_id":"n647af39dfa7c8d05","priority":0.2314,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A concurrent test or system process grabs the just-freed ephemeral port on a busy CI host before the closed-listener probe runs."],"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8571,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_ent